\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  cbank to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2 , sell dlrs at 7.65  \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets   \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2009\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, July 1 (Reuters) - Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was ready to sell dollars at 7.65 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the interbank market on Wednesday to meet growing demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank took its action as Ukrainian officials proceeded with talks with the International Monetary Fund to secure the release of a third tranche of a $16.4 billion loan programme.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said pressure was building up on the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  from several sources -- including seasonal factors, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation and a fresh bout of political uncertainty in the run-up to a presidential election next January.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slipped in the past week in trading on the interbank market to 7.77/7.787 to the dollar from 7.6.\par \par \uc2 In its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, the bank sold dollars at 7.64 and was ready to buy them at 7.61. On Wednesday, it was offering the same purchase rate as the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market on Wednesdays but conducts selected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions for a limited number of individuals.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also removed restrictions for purchasing dollars -- previously limited to buyers for payment for imports contracts and foreign debt within strict deadlines.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank was auctioning dollars at 7.6405 compared to 7.63 -- the rate that has prevailed over the past month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been engaged in nearly daily action -- selective \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions -- since last year to prop the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which lost about 60 percent of its value at one point, falling to 9.0 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 IMPORTS, SPECULATION\par \par \uc2 Dealers said much of the demand for dollars was linked to import contracts. But speculation also played a role.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current weakness is seasonal in nature linked to demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the end of the quarter," said Oleksander Pecherytsin of ING bank.\par \par \uc2 But Oleksander Okhrimenko of Ukrgazbank said speculators were trying more and more to frighten the market through a hyperdevaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Such forecasts have a single aim -- to jolt the market, instal panic and allow speculators who got stuck with dollars at 8-9 to shed expensive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Volodymyr Stelmakh told Reuters last week that the interbank market was subject to a "fragile balance" supported by the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  but subject to disruption at the start of the presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has been gripped by political turmoil since pro-Western politicians took power in the 2004 "Orange Revolution". Most upheavals have focused on policy rows pitting President Viktor Yushchenko against his estranged ally, Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped to $215.7 million in May from $946.3 million in April -- monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of the year exceeded $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 And bank officials had suggested for a time that an improvement in the foreign trade balance in June would lead to further declines in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.\par \par \uc2 But dealers said uncertainty surrounding the election was already prompting outward capital flows to offshore zones.\par \par \uc2 Dealers also say the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level could be affected by the actions of state energy company Naftogaz which must settle its monthly bills for imported Russian gas in dollars.\par \par \uc2 The company paid more than $600 million in May and must settle its next bill -- at least $250 million, by July 7 -- and a further $1 billion a month later. (Writing by Ron Popeski; Editing by Andy Bruce)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090704e5710005x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
