\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine intervention \highlight\uc2  boosts  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2009\par 09:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2    (Repeats story first published on Monday)\par \par \uc2  (Recasts, adds recent comments from Prime Minister)\par \par \uc2  By Natalya Zinets\par \par \uc2  KIEV, May 25 (Reuters) - Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week bought more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it sold for the first time in eight months, boosting the country's reserves by $130 million, the bank's top adviser said on Monday.\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is boosting reserves not only through borrowing, not only through the International Monetary Fund, but now through its own \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the interbank market," Valery Lytvytsky said in an interview.\par \par \uc2  "Last week, we bought $133 million and sold $2.6 million."\par \par \uc2  Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in chronic short supply on the interbank market since last October, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a prolonged tailspin. It lost 60 percent of its value by the end of January, though it has since made up some lost ground.\par \par \uc2  Although Ukraine received the initial $4.5 billion tranche of an IMF credit programme last November, reserves fell to $24.5 billion as of April 30 compared to a historic high $38.1 billion as of August 31, 2008.\par \par \uc2  Last week, Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko said the hyrvnia will continue to strengthen thanks to the IMF loan tranche.\par \par \uc2  Over the last three months, however, sharp reductions in imports and sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not only halted the fall but clawed back some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell at one point last December to 9.0, but rebounded to 8.0 in April. It now stands at about 7.6-7.7.\par \par \uc2  "The strengthening of the exchange rate we now see is not based so much on fundamental issues as it is linked to a correction of an excessive devaluation," Lytvytsky said.\par \par \uc2  "For now, this is a psychological and political effect. From the end of February, when political pressure began to ease on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and with the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day, people stopped taking money out of banks and buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2  Last month, the sale of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $946.3 million from $1.077 billion the previous month and $1.73 billion in February.\par \par \uc2  Last October, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record $4.115 billion -- and sales over the fourth quarter hit $10.285 billion.\par \par \uc2  "But fundamental changes are beginning to take hold which could bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further," he said.\par \par \uc2  Ukraine's monthly current account deficit, he said, was nearly in balance in April and capital account figures showed little change compared to the previous month.\par \par \uc2  According to official figures, the current account deficit fell in April to $70 million from $434 million a month earlier. The capital account was all but unchanged at $986 million against $970 million in March.\par \par \uc2  "The inflow of direct investment and an end to the outflow of individual deposits from banks are two elements which can help balance the capital account," Lytvytsky said.\par \par \uc2  "If this continues, last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases on the interbank market will become a solid trend."\par \par \uc2  Lytvytsky said it was vital for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep buying excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market not only to build up reserves and also to prevent rapid strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "I do not recommend any move towards an excessive strenthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as there is an absence of fundamental factors bolstering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2  "If inflation exceeds 15 percent (April year/year), if there is a sizable hidden budget deficit, a 20 percent decline in the economy, what sort of major strengthening can there be? For now, it is a correction."  (Writing by Ron Popeski; Editing by Andy Bruce/Ruth Pitchford)  \par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-RESERVES/ (RPT, UPDATE 1, INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090529e55q0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
