\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  C.Bank cuts  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  in April\b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2009\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (adds details, comment)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, May 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday its spending on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to $946.3 million in April from $1.077 billion in March.\par \par \uc2 Although the bank continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly every day, the amount devoted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been declining steadily since the beginning of the year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadies after losing about 60 percent of its value since last September.\par \par \uc2 A total of $1.729 billion was spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February and $1.611 billion in January.\par \par \uc2 The dip in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears directly linked to a decline in imports after the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value began to slide last year.\par \par \uc2 "There is no demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Importers have significantly decreased volumes of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases," said one dealer from a major Ukrainian bank.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still outstripped supply on the retail market, though the difference was reduced -- with purchases by consumers in April standing at $1.293 billion and sales at $1.128 billion.\par \par \uc2 In March, purchases stood at $1.476 billion and sales at $909 million.\par \par \uc2 The calmer atmosphere on the interbank market has been reflected in a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the bank announced the first change in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate since Feb. 20, offering to sell dollars on the market at 7.75 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It had been offering to sell dollars at 7.9 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy them at 7.7 for over two months. The buy rate remains the same.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it was altering the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official rate for the first time since December 28 -- this would now stand at 7.71 to the dollar instead of 7.7.\par \par \uc2 In accordance with the terms of an International Monetary Fund credit, the bank said it would close the gap between its official rate and the rate on the interbank market to 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The IMF agreed to lend Ukraine $16.4 billion last year to help offset the effects of the world financial crisis, but the flow of credits was halted amid policy differences.\par \par \uc2 After a visit to Kiev by an IMF mission, the Fund's Board of Directors is expected to endorse the release of the second tranche of $2.8 billion. (Reporting by Yuri Kulikov, writing by Ron Popeski, editing by Victoria Main)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090508e5580019u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
