\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  c.bank to continue with  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets   \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2009\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is determined to press ahead with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite a $1.6 billion drop in reserves since the beginning of the year, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> fell sharply at the end of last year reflecting market worries about the health of Ukraine's economy, losing half of its value to the dollar in December despite persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser Valery Lytvytsky said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had already fallen to $29.9 billion from $31.5 billion at the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Last year, we lost reserves for the second time in the past 10 years. And this is continuing. Today reserves are already at $29.9 billion," Lytvytsky told Reuters in a telephone interview.\par \par \uc2 Although its plunge has jolted confidence in the banking system, with some officials warning of failure to repay debt, the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has inched up to around $7.85 against the dollar as panic on the market eased.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund approved a $16.4 billion loan package for Ukraine in November to help the country withstand the financial crisis and Kiev has also come to terms with Moscow in a damaging gas dispute.\par \par \uc2 Lytvytsky said that under its agreement with the IMF, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was allowed to spend $4.5 billion out of its reserves to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the end of March.\par \par \uc2 "(So far this year) we have spent about $875 million on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is less than we first assumed because the market did a lot on its own. We can spend about $4.5 billion to the end of the quarter," he said.\par \par \uc2 Lytvytsky said the bank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure the interbank exchange rate moved in line with the official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, currently at 7.7 to the dollar and reviewed daily.\par \par \uc2 "Today the gap (between the two) is 1.9 percent," he said, adding it should be no more than two percent.\par \par \uc2 Lytvytsky said that with global prices for its key metals exports falling and prices for Russian gas imports growing, it would be hard to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen significantly any time soon.\par \par \uc2 "I think we should be able to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  at at least 8 against the dollar over the next few days, possibly weeks," he said. (Writing by Maria Golovnina; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /CBANK (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090124e51l000ek\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
