\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine's  \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia \highlight\uc2  set for further slide as gas row drags\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Tong   \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2009\par 16:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Ukraine's embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied on Tuesday, but analysts back it to slide further as new year trading gets underway against the backdrop of a gas row with Russia, now entering its third week.\par \par \uc2 Growing strains on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to prop up the exchange rate are also likely to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has halved in value against the dollar in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the unit plummeted 12 percent to 8.79 against the dollar, its biggest intra-day fall in more than a decade. It held at around similar levels on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There's a very serious risk that Ukraine, if it's not already, will turn into an Iceland-kind of meltdown. There is no reason to buy the Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  today or any time soon," Lars Christensen, head emerging markets at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The situation is extremely serious so the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered to be in a freefall."\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the gas contract dispute between Kiev and Moscow is spurring dollar-buying in Ukraine with growing concern over higher gas prices and the economic impact of the two-week Russian gas embargo on Ukrainian industry [ID:nLD708539]\par \par \uc2 At 1300 GMT, the Reuters conversational dealing system put the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  flat at 8.79 while the local-bank quote for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=UA> showed a wider bid/ask level at 7.700/8.210.\par \par \uc2 "Today the rates on the market were 8.75-8.90 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  per dollar and deals were done at around 8.80," said a Kiev trader.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  LIMITS\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction on Tuesday at 7.86 hrvnias [ID:nLD330196], a stronger level than its offer of 8.62 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  per dollar on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has thinned amid trading restrictions introduced by authorities last year but Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards <GFIU> now put the unit at 10.06 to the dollar in a month, 12.5 in six months and 14.25 in a year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters at the end of December saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 7.97 per dollar by the end of 2009 but some say they are already reconsidering forecasts given continuing volatility after it fell to record lows around 10 to the dollar last month.\par \par \uc2 There is still some doubt over the impact of an expected recession and the likelihood of higher gas prices on a struggling Ukrainian economy.\par \par \uc2 Moscow wants Kiev to pay 2-1/2 times more for its gas while Kiev is demanding higher fees for the transit of Russia gas to Europe through Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "A lot depends on what the two countries eventually agree. We're unsure whether higher gas prices will be offset by higher transit fees," said Jon Harrison, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Dresdner Kleinwort\uc2 . He sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sagging to 9.7 against the greenback in six months.\par \par \uc2 The gas dispute compounds a deepening domestic political crisis in Ukraine, which turned to the International Monetary Fund (IMF) in November for a $16.5 billion loan.\par \par \uc2 But the IMF bailout is likely to curb Ukraine's ability to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by drawing on its fast-depleting reserves, which stand at around $31 billion, some $0.3 billion higher than the IMF requirement set quarterly. [ID:nL6498806]\par \par \uc2 "The IMF is not there to finance capital flight. Its loan was meant to cover the shortfall arising from Ukraine's balance of payments," said Citi economist Ali Al-Eyd. "Under the IMF loan agreement, Ukraine has to move towards a flexible exchange rate but they have been slow to meet the conditions."\par \par \uc2 A further \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  slump could trigger widespread foreign debt default among Ukrainian companies, which according to IMF data, face $38.2 billion in external debt obligations this year.\par \par \uc2 For factbox on 2009 bond redemptions see [ID:nL9646359]\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kylie MacLellan in London and Sabina Zawadzki in Kiev; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090113e51d001bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
