\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Analysts cut Ukraine's '08-'09 GDP,  \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia \highlight\uc2  fcasts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets   \par \uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2008\par 11:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Dec 29 (Reuters) - Analysts cut their forecasts for Ukraine's economic growth to 2.4 percent this year and to a contraction of 3.1 percent in 2009, citing global slowdown and domestic debt defaults as the biggest risks, according to a Reuters poll published on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The 10 analysts polled by Reuters also cut their forecast for the rate of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a median of 7.90/$ by the end of this year and to 7.97/$ by the end of 2009, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit record lows earlier this month, having lost half of its value to the dollar in the past four months.\par \par \uc2 "Much will depend, unfortunately, on external factors -- demand for metals, the readiness of foreign investors to invest in financial projects linked to Euro-2012 (soccer tournament)," said Veronika Movchan, of the Institute for Economic Research and Political Consulting.\par \par \uc2 "The key risks are renewed global growth coming no earlier than 2010, a large amount of default in the real economy, the fall of several large banks due to the defaults and financing the budget deficit."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's economy has experienced a sharp downturn in the past two months -- falling 14.4 percent in November year-on-year after industrial output fell 30 percent. A recession next year would put an end to eight years of robust growth averaging 7 percent annually.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  began slipping to the dollar in September as foreign investors began pulling their funds out and continued its slide, weighed down by a fast widening current account deficit and a sharp fall in exports.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent billions trying to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Ukraine has received $4.5 billion of a $16.4 billion loan, although IMF-set reserves targets mean \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot spend it merely on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate stronger than its lowest point two weeks ago, when it touched 10/$, by the end of this year and next. But a divergence in their forecasts -- 7.5-9/$ this year and 6-10/$ for next year underscored political and financial uncertainty underlining the economy in the coming year. Oleksander Valchyshyn of Invest Capital Ukraine said risks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  included "mistakes in government and parliament's decision-making on budgetary and trade policies.\par \par \uc2 "Spending budget funds wastefully, apart from on investment, together with trade protectionism, could accelerate inflation and create new devaluation pressures on the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate."\par \par \uc2 Analysts were also split on next year's current account deficit and trade gap. Some thought imports could sink next year as Ukraine goes through a deep recession.\par \par \uc2 But Kiev has yet to negotiate a price for Russian gas imports next year. Russia has raised these prices steeply since 2005 and they have been the key factor behind both deficits.\par \par \uc2 Below are the results of the survey of analysts at \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2 , Invest Capital Ukraine, the Institute for Economic Research and Political Consulting, \uc2 Concorde Capital\uc2 , Troika Dialogue, \uc2 Ukrsotsbank\uc2 , \uc2 UkrSibbank\uc2 , Foyil Securities, Razumkov Centre and CASE Ukraine. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      median       lowest   highest   govt fcast   2007\par  CPI Dec (m/m)           1.7          1.1       2.0       1.7         2.1\par  CPI 2008               22.0  (21.9)  21.0     25.2      21.9        16.6\par  CPI 2009               15.0  (15.1)  11.6     20.3       9.5         n/a\par  GDP 2008                2.4  (3.8)    1.2      3.4     1.8-2.5       7.6\par  GDP 2009               -3.1  (-1.6)  -1.1     -6.2      +0.4         n/a\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 /$ end-Dec       7.90 (7.04)   7.5      9.0       7.5/$       5.05\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hryvnia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 /$ end-Dec '09   7.97 (7.21)   6.0     10.0       7.5/$       n/a\par  Trade gap 2008 ($bln)  15.2  (15.3)  12.5     18.4      13.29        7.88\par  Trade gap 2009 ($bln)   9.4  (12.6)   2.5     17.5       7.08        n/a\par  C/A gap 2008 ($bln)    11.9  (12.2)   8.2     14.2      15.0         5.92\par C/A gap 2009 ($bln)     5.8  (8.8)    2.5     12.5     0-2 pct/GDP   n/a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Writing by Sabina Zawadzki; editing by Tony Austin)\f28 \par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-ECONOMY (POLL)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090101e4ct0008c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
