\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Ukraine's politics and finances\b0\par\par\uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2008\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko on Wednesday pronounced Ukraine's political crisis over and vowed to strengthen her government team to tackle the impact of the world financial crisis on the country.\par \par \uc2 Following are key facts about Ukraine's politics and finances and why the ex-Soviet state is especially vulnerable to heightened risk aversion among international investors.\par \par \uc2 POLITICS\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine has been plagued by political turbulence since "Orange Revolution" protests in 2004 brought to power President Viktor Yushchenko and a team committed to moving closer to the West and joining NATO and the European Union.\par \par \uc2 Rows pitting Yushchenko against his former ally Yulia Tymoshenko, who has twice served as his prime minister, undermined the "orange" camp and brought down governments.\par \par \uc2 A coalition between parties backed by the two leaders was reinstated on Tuesday, resolving for now the latest deadlock and avoiding a snap parliamentary poll.\par \par \uc2 * Upheaval -- and trouble forming a stable ruling coalition -- reflect Ukraine's longstanding division into the nationalist west and centre, which looks to the EU and United States, and the Russian-speaking east and south, friendlier towards Moscow.\par \par \uc2 * Relations with Russia, bumpy throughout the post-Soviet period, have sunk to unprecedented lows over Yushchenko's denunciation of Moscow's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Georgia. \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  depends heavily on Moscow for energy supplies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICY\par \par \uc2 * The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to sink to a fresh historic low of 7.6-7.66/$ on Wednesday. It has lost over 60 percent of its value since September, weakened by global risk aversion, a lack of confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a drop in exports which bring dollars into the market.\par \par \uc2 * In mid-2008, the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had strengthened as far as 4.5/$, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned a policy of keeping it in a corridor of 5.00-5.06 per dollar, within a 4.95-5.25 band.\par \par \uc2 * The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scrapped a trading band and repeatedly devalued its official rate despite spending $7.7 billion on propping up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  between September-November.\par \par \uc2 FINANCES\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine has received a $4.5 billion tranche from an IMF loan which it secured at the beginning of November. The loan came on condition of fiscal prudence, recapitalisation of banks and a more liberal exchange rate mechanism.\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say the funds should be used as a bridge loan for Ukraine's government, which has been unable to issue Eurobonds this year, and to recapitalise banks. They worry, however, that the funds will be spent on just supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as of the end of November rose to $32.7 billion against $31.9 billion at the end of October, helped by the IMF tranche.\par \par \uc2 * The current account deficit was running at $10.5 billion, or 6.5 percent of GDP, in the first 10 months of this year. Analysts see the full-year deficit at between $10-14 billion but diverge on next year's forecast, citing between $3-$20 billion due to uncertainties over gas prices and the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate.\par \par \uc2 * Prices and demand for steel exports have dropped, while Russia's \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  has suggested next year's price for gas imports could soar to $400 per 1,000 cubic metres from $179.50 now.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISKS\par \par \uc2 * The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks whittling away its reserves, and encouraging imports and further widening the trade gap, if it supports the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Letting it float freely would introduce uncertainty and speculation, removing an important anchor for domestic and foreign businesses in the export-driven economy. * Many people hold debt in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are having increasing difficulties paying it off as the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens.\par \par \uc2 * Consumers are extremely sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements -- they lost savings when the Soviet Union collapsed and again through hyperinflation and a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in the 1990s that more than halved the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value to about 4/$ and beyond.\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine was forced to restructure its debts in 2000 and made the final payments just last year. Credit default swaps -- a tradeable instrument that measures risks of debt default -- have consistently been some of the highest for emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 FOREIGN DEBT\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine's foreign debt totalled just over $100 billion as of July 1, of which about $15 billion was government debt.\par \par \uc2 * A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official has said companies and banks must pay off $5 billion in debts by the end of this year and another $30 billion next year.\par \par \uc2 * Analysts estimate Ukraine's 2009 external financing requirement to be $55-66 billion, of which $32-40 billion is in the private sector. Foreign banks own 40-42 percent of total banking assets and 25 percent of short-term banking debt is owed to parent banks. (Compiled by Sabina Zawadzki, editing by Mark Trevelyan)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-CRISIS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|RNA|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081210e4ca000wf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
