\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-IMF approves $16.5 bln Ukraine loan, first tranche\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lesley Wroughton and Sabina Zawadzki   \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2008\par 02:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments by head of Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON/KIEV, Nov 6 (Reuters) - The IMF approved a $16.5 billion loan program for Ukraine to ease strains from the global financial crisis and officials expressed confidence on Thursday that the country was out of the woods.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund, in a statement issued late on Wednesday, said it would immediately disburse $4.5 billion to the government under the two-year loan agreement.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities' programme is designed to help stabilise the domestic financial system against a backdrop of global deleveraging and a domestic crisis of confidence, and to facilitate adjustment of the economy to a large terms-of-trade shock," the Fund said.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities' plan incorporates monetary and exchange rate policy shifts, banking recapitalization, and fiscal and incomes policy adjustments."\par \par \uc2 In Kiev, President Viktor Yushchenko welcomed the decision, taken after Ukraine's fractious parliament approved enabling legislation. He said it provided a "signal to the international community to boost the rating of trust in our country".\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko, the president's former ally turned rival, said the loan would "allow us to stabilise completely the financial situation in Ukraine".\par \par \uc2 The head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-making council later said it was the bank's open-ended \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that stabilised the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it hit a historic low last week.\par \par \uc2 Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of up to $500 million, Petro Poroshenko said, had reduced demand for dollars and restored stability.\par \par \uc2 "Exports had earlier held foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation of a higher (dollar) rate and dealers had earlier tried to make money on working the rates. But today we have a significantly reduced scope for speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko also said subsequent tranches of the IMF loan would depend on whether authorities formally requested them.\par \par \uc2 Disbursement of parts of IMF credits is generally linked to further review of conditions in the country receiving the funds.\par \par \uc2 RECESSION FORECAST\par \par \uc2 The IMF decision was issued along with forecast indicators predicting that Ukraine would sink into recession next year, with a 3 percent fall against 6 percent growth this year.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Murilo Portugal, IMF deputy managing director, said Ukraine's economy, especially its banking system, was under severe stress, caused by a drop in global steel prices, the country's main export, and global financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 He said Ukraine's programme would seek to restore financial and economic stability through a more flexible exchange rate regime with targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so-called 'pre-emptive' recapitalisation of banks, and tighter monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "The flexible exchange rate regime, backed by an appropriate monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will help absorb external shocks and avoid disorderly exchange market developments," Portugal said.\par \par \uc2 "The recent unification of official and market exchange rates should increase clarity about the regime."\par \par \uc2 Exchange controls recently imposed, he said, would be phased out as confidence returns to the economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early October to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It began offering buy-sell rates for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week after previously only selling or buying a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Portugal said as credit pressures abate, tighter monetary policy will be needed to guard against inflation.\par \par \uc2 He said the government's target of a balanced 2009 budget would be reviewed, although it could be achieved through expenditure restraint and a phased increase in energy tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Portugal said recapitalisation efforts for banks would ease liquidity pressures that could prolong an economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 "Decisive measures that have been taken to allocate public funds to recapitalise banks and to facilitate bank resolution processes will ensure that problems can be dealt with promptly," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 IMF-UKRAINE/ (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|BNX|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081106e4b60002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
