\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Serbia to trim budget, Ukraine seeks IMF okay\b0\par\par\uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2008\par 19:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Serbia promises to cut spending for IMF support\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine awaits approval of $16.5 billion bailout\par \par \uc2 * EBRD warns of recession in previously fast-growing ex-communist Europe\par \par \uc2 By Gordana Filipovic\par \par \uc2 BELGRADE, Nov 4 (Reuters) - Serbia said on Tuesday it would cut its 2009 budget to get a safety line from the IMF and Ukraine appealed to the fund for approval of a $16.5 billion rescue plan to weather the global financial storm.\par \par \uc2 They were the latest central and eastern European states to look to the International Monetary Fund for help as the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development [EBRD.UL] warned a number of countries in the region were sure to hit recession.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say that Serbia, a small but open economy heavily reliant on external borrowing and investment, is not as exposed to the global crisis as Iceland, Ukraine or Hungary, hit by market turmoil in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Yet Serbia's dinar was the biggest loser in an investor flight from the emerging European assets last month that has raised fears that economies in the Balkans and on the Baltic Sea could hit trouble as the crisis filters through.\par \par \uc2 "We are still in talks with the International Monetary Fund about the (2009) budget," Deputy Prime Minister Mladjan Dinkic said. "Our position is to have the precautionary programme and not to draw the money unless it is really necessary."\par \par \uc2 A top government official said later in the day the IMF was demanding that Belgrade cut its fiscal gap to 1.5 percent of GDP from 2.7 percent this year by freezing real wages and pensions.\par \par \uc2 Both spoke a day after the Finance Ministry said Serbia was negotiating a stand-by deal with the IMF, with analysts estimating the country's needs up to two billion euros in a worst case scenario if global financial turmoil triggered panic.\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE APPEALS\par \par \uc2 "As a consequence of the financial crisis, it is now clear that much of the region faces major challenges and recession in a number of countries is already certain," EBRD chief Thomas Mirow told the European Parliament on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Tuesday, Ukraine's President Viktor Yushchenko told IMF Executive Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn he was "counting on support from the IMF to overcome the effects of the world financial crisis on the country's economy".\par \par \uc2 After days of domestic political bickering, the country's parliament last week backed a package of laws to stabilise the country's finances and the banking system, vital to win the IMF financial backing.\par \par \uc2 The IMF's board was due to consider the credit in a meeting in Washington later on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Yushchenko also called for help from the World Bank, which is considering a $500 million credit for \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis for two weeks to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it hit a record low to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Just months ago, a new pro-western government, fast-expanding economy and a firm and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also bolstered hopes Serbia was set to catch up with its more prosperous neighbours.\par \par \uc2 Officials have repeatedly dismissed the need for the country to reach out to the IMF, but agree that its backing could be a welcome signal to investors that reforms will stay on track as the Balkan country plans to apply for EU membership next year.\par \par \uc2 Jurij Bajec, economic advisor to the Serbian prime minister, said both the IMF and the Serbian government seemed concerned the impact of the global crisis could be more serious than initially thought.\par \par \uc2 "On one hand, there is a problem to finance the budget deficit," Bajec told Reuters. "On the other hand, the IMF is concerned about Serbia's external vulnerability and ability to finance 500-600 million euros a month in external liabilities."\par \par \uc2 Serbia's commitment to belt-tightening may be challenged in parliament, where the opposition is demanding that the ruling coalition meet all pre-election pledges, including higher wages and pensions.\par \par \uc2 "Serbia has two alternatives. One is to do nothing and let the public spending and the current account deficit grow, which lead to inflation and the adjustment in the exchange rate," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Radovan Jelasic told an investor forum. "The alternative is to freeze spending." (Additional reporting by Ivana Sekularac in Belgrade and Ron Popeski in Kiev; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 CENTRALEUROPE-FINANCIAL/ (WRAPUP)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|BNX|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081104e4b4002i6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
