\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Ukraine Central Banker: Won't Fight Hyrvnia Trend\b0\par\par\uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2008\par 17:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more quotes on inflation, slowdown)\par \par \uc2 By Alexander Kolyandr\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 KIEV (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to "combat the trend" if the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens, the chairman of the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2 's council said Friday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires, Petro Poroshenko said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act to smooth sharp moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and will accept moves of 10% up or down against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko said Ukraine has $38 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, enough to cover import for 4 months.\par \par \uc2 He said that those reserves will not be used to "combat the trend of the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "We don't exclude that the National Bank will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises sharply," he said. "We have enough reserves to flatten peaks, but are not going to fight the trend.".\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 12 percent against the dollar since the beginning of September.\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko said the NBU will be selective if there is a need to support the country's banking system, providing liquidity if needed, but not bailing out banks that have made bad lending decisions.\par \par \uc2 "We have no aim to save all the banks," he said. "If the root of a problem of a bank is a lack of liquidity, we have no problems with refinancing it, but if the root of the problem is imprudent credit policy, a sharp rise in defaulted borrowers, it's bank's own problems and taxpayers shouldn't pay for unprofessional management of the bank."\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko said the level of deposit insurance may be increased. It now stands at 50,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which Poroshenko said covers about 90% of deposits. He said it may be increased to include up to 96% of all the country's deposits.\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko said the added uncertainty that a fresh round of elections in December will bring could be the trigger for problems in the banking system.\par \par \uc2 "The election campaign may provoke a run on banks, but even in this case the National Bank has instruments to stabilize deposit situation," he said.\par \par \uc2 He also said that the government should take swift and strict measures to combat the financial crisis. He said that a balanced budget and "a pragmatic social policy" are needed for 2009 and privatization of telecommunications, power generating and petrochemical companies should be "unblocked".\par \par \uc2 He also said that the ban on selling land should be lifted, as it would attract investment in Ukraine's agriculture. Poroshenko said that Ukraine's banking system "feels better than the systems of many Western countries and Russia", as the country "has no complicated instruments, no derivatives, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange policy isn't fully liberalized and low ratings by the rating agencies prevented companies from over-borrowing".\par \par \uc2 He accepted that the country suffers from a liquidity problem, but said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has instruments and reserves to manage liquidity and keep stability".\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko admitted that there is already a slowdown in Ukraine's construction, real estate and metal sectors. The latter provides a lion share of the country's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the fall in metal prices "doesn't put the country's trade balance in danger, as it will be offset by the drop in prices of oil", which Ukraine imports.\par \par \uc2 Poroshenko also said that he expects inflation to reach 19% to 19.5% by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 -By Alexander Kolyandr, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 20 7842 9410; alexander.kolyandr@dowjones.com [ 10-10-08 1555GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081010e4aa000te\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
