\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine C.Bank purchases push reserves to $36 bln\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets   \par \uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2008\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, July 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars on the interbank market have pushed reserves up to $36 billion, a senior bank official said on Friday, against a figure of $35.439 billion less than two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 "We had reserves of $32 billion at the beginning of the year and this figure is now $36 billion," Valery Lytvytsky, a senior adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, said by telephone.\par \par \uc2 "We have already bought $1 billion in July, plus $1 billion in June and nearly another $1 billion in May and a bit more in the first quarter. We have increased our reserves by $4 billion since the New Year.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement last week put reserves at $35.439 as of June 30 against $32.462 at the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday bought dollars on the interbank market at 4.74 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  against 4.72 on Monday, the last time it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lytvytsky said inflows of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's domestic market had risen sharply in the past six months owing in part to increased exports -- suggesting a pending improvement in the foreign trade and current account balances.\par \par \uc2 "There is a growing trend towards improvement in the trade and current account balances. In April, the current account deficit stood at $1.7 billion, while in May it shrank by half to $800 million," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Given the way the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going, we believe that in June-July, exports will rise faster than imports and the current account will shrink.\par \par \uc2 "We believe prices are so high for metals, fertiliser and agricultural goods that the strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have no negative impact on exports and there will be a positive effect on imports, with energy becoming cheaper."\par \par \uc2 Figures released this week by the State Statistics Committee showed month-on-month inflation declined to 0.8 percent in June from 1.3 percent in May and a peak of 3.8 percent in March.\par \par \uc2 "For the moment, we see that our exchange rate policy is the right one," Lytvytsky said.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May to 4.85 to the dollar after maintaining it within a corridor of 5.0-5.06 for nearly three years. It has since made minor adjustments to the official rate, which on Friday stood at 4.8417.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman Volodymyr Stelmakh and the head of the bank's council, Petro Poroshenko, last week said they had agreed to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official rate within 4 percent of 4.85 over the second half of the year. (Writing by Ron Popeski; Editing by Victoria Main)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE-RESERVES/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080711e47b000so\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
