\par\par \b \uc2 UDPATE 1-Ukraine cbank sells dlrs for 6th straight day   \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2008\par 16:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (adds new comment, background)   \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the sixth straight trading day on Tuesday despite few imbalances on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 "We have been stuck over the past couple of days at about the level offered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one dealer. "The bank is selling dollars without any problem. There is no urgency as the market is pretty calm."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a tight band of 5.00-5.06 to the dollar since August 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Most transactions over the past two days stood at about 5.0635/5.065. But last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to a 16-month low as investors scrambled for dollars to shore up losses from global market volatility.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is calmer, but the situation remains unsteady," said another dealer. "It is possible that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to come onto the market we could again see things go to 5.07."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars practically throughout 2007, with the exception of December, to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the narrow band.   \par \par \uc2 Over the year, with dollar supply outstripping demand, the bank purchased the equivalent of $7.9 billion on the interbank market, mostly in euros.   \par \par \uc2 Given a growing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deficit, dealers believe that demand by importers for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will exceed supply by exporters.   \par \par \uc2 This, however, could be offset by an inflow of funds from privatisation of large enterprises, like fixed-line telephone operator  \uc2 Ukrtelekom\uc2  or the Odessa Pre-Port chemical plant.   \par \par \uc2 "The inflow of direct foreign investment is estimated at $7 billion to $10 billion and this could cover the trade deficit and boost \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply," said one dealer.   \par \par \uc2 "But this is likely to happen closer to the summer and be a factor in strengthening the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (Reporting by Natalia Zinets, writing by Ron Popeski)   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080201e41t001k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
